WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.005
Authentication for remote servers.

2
00:00:02.285 --> 00:00:07.747
Authorization in MCP is optional, and the specification scopes it by transport.

3
00:00:08.027 --> 00:00:12.656
Servers on an HTTP transport should follow the authorization section.

4
00:00:12.936 --> 00:00:17.971
Servers on stdio should not, and should take credentials from the environment instead.

5
00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:29.813
The MCP tutorial recommends authorization whenever a server handles user-specific data, needs an audit trail of who did what, or fronts APIs that need user consent.

6
00:00:30.093 --> 00:00:31.544
The model is OAuth 2.

7
00:00:31.824 --> 00:00:34.149
1, with roles you may know from web work.

8
00:00:34.429 --> 00:00:39.763
The MCP server is a resource server: it accepts and validates access tokens.

9
00:00:40.043 --> 00:00:43.861
The MCP client is an OAuth client acting for the user.

10
00:00:44.141 --> 00:00:48.621
A separate authorization server interacts with the user and issues tokens.

11
00:00:48.901 --> 00:00:59.888
Servers must publish OAuth Protected Resource Metadata (RFC 9728), which is how a client discovers which authorization server to use.

12
00:01:00.168 --> 00:01:07.123
Clients send the token in the Authorization: Bearer header on every request, and never in a URL query string.

13
00:01:07.403 --> 00:01:09.771
The server-side rules are short and strict.

14
00:01:10.051 --> 00:01:19.523
Servers must validate tokens, and must check that each token was issued for them as its intended audience (RFC 8707).

15
00:01:19.803 --> 00:01:23.963
Invalid or expired tokens get HTTP 401.

16
00:01:24.243 --> 00:01:31.155
Servers must only accept tokens valid for their own resources, and must not accept or transit any other tokens.

17
00:01:31.435 --> 00:01:38.347
That last rule forbids token passthrough: taking a token from the client and forwarding it to a downstream API.

18
00:01:38.627 --> 00:01:41.720
The security page explains why it is dangerous.

19
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:55.525
The downstream API may trust the token as if your server had validated it (the confused deputy problem), its rate limits and audit logs see the wrong identity, and a stolen token can use your server as a proxy.

20
00:01:55.805 --> 00:02:00.435
If your server calls another API, it gets its own token for that API.

21
00:02:00.715 --> 00:02:03.744
Two practical points from the MCP tutorial.

22
00:02:04.024 --> 00:02:12.856
Use an existing identity provider or authorization server rather than writing your own, and check that it can bind the audience to your server specifically.

23
00:02:13.136 --> 00:02:16.272
And use well-tested libraries for token validation.

24
00:02:16.552 --> 00:02:31.080
In the 2026-07-28 revision, Client ID Metadata Documents are the preferred way for clients to register, and Dynamic Client Registration is deprecated but kept for compatibility.
